To comply with the C++ Core Guidelines (C.65: 'Make move assignment safe for self-assignment') and prevent potential undefined behavior or resource leaks during self-move-assignment (e.g., `m = std::move(m)`), it is highly recommended to add a self-assignment check `if (this != &other)` in the move assignment operator.

```c
    ordered_map& operator=(ordered_map&& other) noexcept(std::is_nothrow_move_assignable<Container>::value)
    {
        if (this != &other)
        {
            Container::operator=(std::move(static_cast<Container&>(other)));
        }
        return *this;
    }
```

![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

To comply with the C++ Core Guidelines (C.65: 'Make move assignment safe for self-assignment') and prevent potential undefined behavior or resource leaks during self-move-assignment (e.g., `m = std::move(m)`), it is highly recommended to add a self-assignment check `if (this != &other)` in the move assignment operator.

```c
    ordered_map& operator=(ordered_map&& other) noexcept(std::is_nothrow_move_assignable<Container>::value)
    {
        if (this != &other)
        {
            Container::operator=(std::move(static_cast<Container&>(other)));
        }
        return *this;
    }
```

The copy-assignment is made self-assignment safe (and even has a dedicated test for it), but the move-assignment has no `this != &other` guard. `m = std::move(m)` therefore performs a self-move on the underlying `std::vector`, which the standard leaves in a *valid but unspecified* state — in practice it can end up empty, silently discarding the contents.

This isn't reachable through the `WITH_DEFAULT` ternary that motivated the fix (that always materializes a distinct temporary), so severity is low, but given the asymmetry with the copy operator and the self-assignment test you added, it'd be worth either guarding it or adding a self-move test to document the intended behavior:

```suggestion
    ordered_map& operator=(ordered_map&& other) noexcept(std::is_nothrow_move_assignable<Container>::value)
    {
        if (this != &other)
        {
            Container::operator=(std::move(static_cast<Container&>(other)));
        }
        return *this;
    }
```

![critical](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/critical.svg)

There are two critical issues here:
1. **Crash Vulnerability**: For Memcached connections, `msg.handle` holds `MCPipelineMessagePtr` instead of `PipelineMessagePtr`. Calling `std::get<Connection::PipelineMessagePtr>(msg.handle)` will throw `std::bad_variant_access` and crash the server. We must restrict disk backpressure to `Protocol::REDIS`.
2. **Idempotency Bug**: If `Init()` fails or `HasEnoughBackingSpace` is false, `handle()` is called but `false` is returned. This causes `DispatchSingle` to call `cmd_msg_cb()` again. In `ParseMemcache`, `cmd_msg_cb` moves `cmd` (`std::move(cmd)`), so the second call will operate on a moved-from state, leading to undefined behavior or empty commands.

To fix both, we should only attempt offloading for `Protocol::REDIS`, and if offloading fails after constructing the message, we should fallback to `SendAsync` and return `true` (optionally blocking if limits are exceeded) instead of returning `false`.

```c
bool Connection::OffloadBackpressureToDiskIfNeeded(absl::FunctionRef<MessageHandle()> handle) {
  // Offload only when dispatch_q_ crosses watermark or when backing queue already
  // has pending items.
  if (protocol_ == Protocol::REDIS && backing_queue_ &&
      ((dispatch_q_.size() > backing_queue_->Watermark()) || !backing_queue_->Empty())) {
    auto ec = backing_queue_->Init();
    LOG_IF(ERROR, ec) << "Failed to init disk backed backpressure with error " << ec.message();

    if (!ec) {
      MessageHandle msg = handle();
      PipelineMessage* pmsg = std::get<Connection::PipelineMessagePtr>(msg.handle).get();
      if (backing_queue_->HasEnoughBackingSpace(pmsg)) {
        auto write_ec = backing_queue_->OffloadToBacking(pmsg);
        if (!write_ec) {
          if (dispatch_q_.size() == 0) {
            disk_backpressure_available_ = true;
            cnd_.notify_one();
          }
          // Recycle message
          QueueBackpressure& qbp = GetQueueBackpressure();
          if (stats_->pipeline_cmd_cache_bytes < qbp.pipeline_cache_limit) {
            stats_->pipeline_cmd_cache_bytes += pmsg->StorageCapacity();
            pipeline_req_pool_.push_back(
                std::move(std::get<Connection::PipelineMessagePtr>(msg.handle)));
          }
          return true;
        }
        LOG(ERROR) << "Failed to write backpressure to disk: " << write_ec.message();
      } else {
        LOG(WARNING) << "Disk backpressure file size limit reached. Could not offload backpressure.";
      }
      QueueBackpressure& qbp = GetQueueBackpressure();
      if (qbp.IsPipelineBufferOverLimit(stats_->dispatch_queue_bytes, dispatch_q_.size())) {
        stats_->pipeline_throttle_count++;
        fb2::NoOpLock noop;
        qbp.pipeline_cnd.wait(noop, [this, &qbp] {
          bool over_limits =
              qbp.IsPipelineBufferOverLimit(stats_->dispatch_queue_bytes, dispatch_q_.size());
          return !over_limits || (dispatch_q_.empty() && !cc_->async_dispatch) || cc_->conn_closing;
        });
        if (cc_->conn_closing) {
          if (request_shutdown_) {
            ShutdownSelfBlocking();
          }
          return true;
        }
      }
      SendAsync(std::move(msg));
      return true;
    }
  }
  return false;
}
```

![critical](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/critical.svg)

`OffloadToBacking` currently returns `void` and silently ignores write errors from `writer_->Write`. The caller assumes success and recycles the message, leading to silent data loss where client commands are completely lost. Return `std::error_code` instead.

```c
std::error_code Connection::DiskBackedBackpressureQueue::OffloadToBacking(
    const Connection::PipelineMessage* msg) {
  ItemOffset item;
  item.offset = next_offset_;
  item.total_bytes = msg->FullCommand().size();

  size_t start = absl::GetCurrentTimeNanos();

  // TODO we should truncate as the file grows. That way we never end up with large files
  // on disk.
  auto res = writer_->Write(msg->FullCommand());
  if (res) {
    LOG(ERROR) << "Failed to offload connection " << this << " backpressure with offset "
               << item.offset << " of size " << item.total_bytes << " to backing with error: " << res;
    return res;
  }

  // Only update for non error paths
  size_t end = absl::GetCurrentTimeNanos();
  max_io_write_latency_ = std::max(max_io_write_latency_, (end - start));

  total_backing_bytes_ += msg->FullCommand().size();
  offsets_.push_back(item);
  next_offset_ += item.total_bytes;

  VLOG(2) << "Offload connection " << this << " backpressure of " << item.total_bytes
          << " bytes to disk at offset: " << item.offset;
  VLOG(3) << "Command offloaded: " << msg->FullCommand();
  return {};
}
```

