Using `reader.readline()` in a loop to drain 1,000,000 lines is extremely slow in Python. Using `reader.read(1024 * 1024)` is orders of magnitude faster and prevents test timeouts.

```suggestion
    while True:
        try:
            response = await asyncio.wait_for(reader.read(1024 * 1024), timeout=2)
            if not response:
                break
        except asyncio.TimeoutError:
            break
```

**Crash on memcache connections.** `DispatchSingle` is also called from `ParseMemcache` (line 1364), where `cmd_msg_cb` returns an `MCPipelineMessage` (see line 1325-1327), not a `PipelineMessage`. When `backing_queue_` is enabled and a memcache connection crosses the watermark, `std::get<Connection::PipelineMessagePtr>(msg.handle)` will throw `std::bad_variant_access` (or `std::terminate`/abort if exceptions are disabled) — a remotely-triggerable server crash.

You need to guard on the variant actually holding a `PipelineMessagePtr` before offloading, e.g.:

```cpp
msg = handle();
auto* pmsg_ptr = std::get_if<Connection::PipelineMessagePtr>(&msg.handle);
if (!pmsg_ptr) {
  // Not a plain pipeline message (e.g. memcache / control msg) — fall back to normal dispatch.
  SendAsync(std::move(msg));
  return true;
}
```

(Note: if you fall back to `SendAsync` here you must still consume `msg` so it isn't re-created by the caller.)

![critical](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/critical.svg)

Using `continue` here skips the increment of `s_current` (which happens at the end of the `while` loop on line 1305), leading to an infinite loop. You should increment `s_current` before continuing, or restructure the loop to avoid `continue`.

```c
                if (edges.first == edges.second) {
                  s_current += distancebetweentrees;
                  continue;
                }
```

**Infinite loop risk:** `continue` here jumps straight back to the `while (s_current < s_end)` condition without executing `s_current += distancebetweentrees;` at the bottom of the loop. If `lane->GetWidth(s_current) != 0.0f` is true but `edges.first == edges.second`, `s_current` never advances and this loop spins forever (hanging mesh/tree generation).

The guard should still advance `s_current` before skipping:

```suggestion
                if (edges.first == edges.second) {
                  s_current += distancebetweentrees;
                  continue;
                }
```

(Note: the guard implies degenerate edges *can* occur even when `GetWidth != 0`, so this is reachable, not just theoretical.)

