The `cluster-announce-ip` configuration is defined with the `EMPTY_STRING_IS_NULL` flag. This means that if a user resets or sets the configuration to an empty string (e.g., `CONFIG SET cluster-announce-ip ""`), `val` will be passed as `NULL` to `isValidClusterAnnounceIp`.

Calling `sdslen(val)` when `val` is `NULL` will result in a NULL pointer dereference and crash the server. We should add a safety check to return `1` (valid) if `val` is `NULL`.

```c
static int isValidClusterAnnounceIp(char *val, const char **err) {
    if (val == NULL) return 1;
    if (!(isValidAuxString(val, sdslen(val)))) {
        *err = "cluster-announce-ip contained invalid character";
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}
```

The new control-character guard correctly uses `ch` (the `unsigned char`), but `isalnum(c)` and `strchr(invalid_charset, c)` are still passed the original `int c`. The callers feed bytes from `isValidAuxString` via `s[i]` where `s` is `char *`, so on platforms where `char` is signed, any byte ≥ 0x80 (e.g. UTF‑8 nodenames set via `cluster-announce-human-nodename`) arrives as a negative `int`. Passing a negative value other than `EOF` to `isalnum()` is undefined behavior (CERT STR37‑C), and `strchr` will reinterpret it. Since you already have the sanitized `ch`, use it consistently:

```suggestion
    /* Allow alphanumeric characters. */
    if (isalnum(ch)) return 1;

    /* Reject characters that are format-significant in nodes.conf or could
     * break parsing: delimiters, quotes, backslash, and other specials. */
    static const char *invalid_charset = "!#$%&()*+,;<=>?@[]^{|}~\"'\\ ";

    return strchr(invalid_charset, ch) == NULL;
```

